1
00:00:17,351 --> 00:00:21,213
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is Majjhima Nikaya 21, just a section of it.

2
00:00:23,153 --> 00:00:29,916
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose that a man were to come along carrying a spade and a basket saying, I will make this great earth be without earth.

3
00:00:30,656 --> 00:00:36,279
[SPEAKER_03]: He would dig here and there, scatter the soil here and there, spit here and there, urinate here and there.

4
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:41,721
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know why someone would do that.

5
00:00:42,061 --> 00:00:43,842
[SPEAKER_03]: Be without earth, he would say.

6
00:00:43,982 --> 00:00:44,642
[SPEAKER_03]: Be without earth.

7
00:00:45,732 --> 00:00:46,533
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, what do you think?

8
00:00:47,233 --> 00:00:51,296
[SPEAKER_03]: Would he make this great earth be without earth?

9
00:00:51,356 --> 00:00:52,557
[SPEAKER_03]: And they say, no, Lord.

10
00:00:53,057 --> 00:00:53,517
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that?

11
00:00:53,557 --> 00:00:55,579
[SPEAKER_03]: Because this earth is immeasurable.

12
00:00:56,279 --> 00:00:58,320
[SPEAKER_03]: It can't easily be made without earth.

13
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:00,802
[SPEAKER_03]: The man would reap only disappointment.

14
00:01:03,003 --> 00:01:10,108
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, in the same way, there are these five aspects of speech by which others may address you.

15
00:01:11,429 --> 00:01:14,291
[SPEAKER_03]: Timely or untimely, true or false,

16
00:01:15,553 --> 00:01:28,301
[SPEAKER_03]: Gentle or harsh, beneficial or unbeneficial, with a mind of friendliness or hate, and in... that's five... in any event, you should train yourselves.

17
00:01:29,882 --> 00:01:34,444
[SPEAKER_03]: Our minds will not be overturned, and we will say no evil words.

18
00:01:35,605 --> 00:01:40,628
[SPEAKER_03]: With a mind of friendliness, without hate, we will have pity for that person.

19
00:01:41,369 --> 00:01:43,350
[SPEAKER_03]: We will regard him with a friendly mind,

20
00:01:44,092 --> 00:01:54,776
[SPEAKER_03]: And with that as a foundation, we will regard the whole world with a mind of friendliness that is abundant, expansive, immeasurable, free from hostility.

21
00:01:55,756 --> 00:01:56,656
[SPEAKER_03]: That's how you should train.

22
00:01:57,456 --> 00:01:59,057
[SPEAKER_03]: So, be like that earth.

23
00:01:59,677 --> 00:02:07,820
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I think the question was how to be like that earth.

24
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:08,440
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a question?

25
00:02:08,817 --> 00:02:16,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, like, the composure should be measurable to the point where, whichever way, whoever addresses one...

26
00:02:16,804 --> 00:02:19,066
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind, the mind should be measurable.

27
00:02:19,086 --> 00:02:22,089
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just a question of you doing the composure.

28
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:26,873
[SPEAKER_01]: You do the composure that then sets the mind up, so to speak.

29
00:02:27,333 --> 00:02:33,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Makes it turn away from things, makes it immeasurable by things, but you don't have direct access to the mind.

30
00:02:34,019 --> 00:02:35,481
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just make it immeasurable.

31
00:02:36,627 --> 00:02:41,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole first jhāna practice that we spoke before.

32
00:02:44,433 --> 00:03:05,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind would turn away from vittaka vichara, from thinking and pondering, from movements of your thoughts, from comings and goings of your thoughts, from rising or passing away of the aggregates, by composing the context of danger, peril, lack of satisfaction, not worth my time, not worth engaging, all these

33
00:03:06,601 --> 00:03:11,605
[SPEAKER_01]: There are a myriad of aspects you can compose your mind on, and then cultivate them.

34
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:18,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Make sure they endure, make sure you protect them through sense of strength and virtue, and the mind will have to turn away.

35
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:19,971
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind will have to lose the interest.

36
00:03:20,071 --> 00:03:21,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you recognize that.

37
00:03:21,633 --> 00:03:23,234
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the skill trainer.

38
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:27,998
[SPEAKER_01]: He notices, ah, when I do this, this is what happens to the mind.

39
00:03:28,078 --> 00:03:32,521
[SPEAKER_01]: When I do that, when I compose something else, or let it do this, this is what happens to the mind.

40
00:03:33,282 --> 00:03:33,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

41
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:38,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, as the Buddha said in that other sutta to Rāhula about the same thing, be like Earth.

42
00:03:39,212 --> 00:03:54,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So, dwell and compose themes, contexts, values of Earth, of immovable, does not... is not affected by dirt people, by spit, by whatever those people do.

43
00:03:54,999 --> 00:03:56,620
[SPEAKER_01]: It just makes no difference.

44
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:02,943
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by dwelling on those contexts, the mind can then become like that.

45
00:04:03,901 --> 00:04:05,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's indirect, in a sense.

46
00:04:05,441 --> 00:04:16,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the reason why people's minds do not become like that, no matter how much... Well, if they dwell on the context, but the minds are not becoming like that, there can only be one reason for that.

47
00:04:17,105 --> 00:04:24,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Minds, they themselves, are still not withdrawn from sensuality, not withdrawn from carelessness,

48
00:04:26,417 --> 00:04:37,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Idle kind of activities, talk, distraction, that keeps the mind still connected, so to speak, keeps the mind engaged with things that are perverting the order.

49
00:04:38,424 --> 00:04:40,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind is not trained, it's not developed in that sense.

50
00:04:41,565 --> 00:05:02,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say, oh, but I'm doing everything rightly, I am fully withdrawn, my virtue is intact, I'm accomplished in virtue, accomplished in seclusion, accomplished in withdrawal from unwholesome states, and I contemplate and I compose the context of great earth, immovability, immeasurability, but my mind is still not turning away.

51
00:05:02,070 --> 00:05:02,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

52
00:05:03,190 --> 00:05:03,790
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

53
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:04,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It would have to.

54
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:08,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It might not do immediately, but it would have to turn away, and then you would know it.

55
00:05:09,172 --> 00:05:18,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole point of the simile here, it's like, yeah, so, make sure that your mind is already measurable like that earth.

56
00:05:19,423 --> 00:05:24,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Make sure that that context... and that's how you would protect the context and see the danger of losing it.

57
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:26,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you just think the mind is on the level of that context, that's it.

58
00:05:26,951 --> 00:05:28,233
[SPEAKER_01]: What I compose, that's what it is.

59
00:05:29,403 --> 00:05:40,933
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not necessarily have perspective in regard to it, which means, when you kind of have different desires or want to do something different, the mind might just go ahead with it.

60
00:05:40,993 --> 00:05:41,733
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually...

61
00:05:42,154 --> 00:05:49,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait, but this context I'm protecting now, I don't feel any danger in losing it, because

62
00:05:50,863 --> 00:05:52,063
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll deal with it, so to speak.

63
00:05:52,623 --> 00:05:54,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, if this context changes, I'll deal with it.

64
00:05:54,664 --> 00:05:56,064
[SPEAKER_01]: I want it changed, and so on.

65
00:05:56,084 --> 00:06:00,625
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's when you ignore how it will affect the mind, for which you don't have a say.

66
00:06:01,205 --> 00:06:04,725
[SPEAKER_01]: You have a say in the context, and how you compose your mind, and what you choose to dwell on.

67
00:06:05,265 --> 00:06:09,706
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't have a say in how the mind will act, and react, and respond to that.

68
00:06:10,106 --> 00:06:11,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's then, you know...

69
00:06:11,287 --> 00:06:19,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although I can handle this context changing, and I can handle thinking about different things, I can handle this, I can handle sensuality, I can handle...

70
00:06:20,366 --> 00:06:21,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's not the point.

71
00:06:22,251 --> 00:06:26,160
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is that the mind will do its own thing then, and you can't handle that.

72
00:06:27,538 --> 00:06:33,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize the danger of losing the context is not whether I can handle or not new thoughts and different thoughts.

73
00:06:34,442 --> 00:06:39,385
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger is, oh, but I know what my mind will do, and that's what I need to protect.

74
00:06:40,126 --> 00:06:42,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can handle sensuality, great.

75
00:06:42,768 --> 00:06:51,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, keep handling it, keep handling yourself away from it, so that then the mind will also not... will lose completely interest towards it.

76
00:06:52,398 --> 00:06:57,243
[SPEAKER_01]: So, be like earth, be like water, be like... be like these elements in that... in that ārasutta.

77
00:06:57,803 --> 00:06:58,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

78
00:06:58,324 --> 00:07:08,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Make your mind become like that, on account of diligent pondering on these contexts, properties, qualities, that will make the mind

79
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:28,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Go that way, pliable, obeying that direction that you're setting up, if you have been withdrawn from sensuality, distraction, idle chatter, obviously acts of ill will, and everything else pretty much, oh, yes, sleep, engaging in pointless work, distracting work, and so on.

80
00:07:29,793 --> 00:07:30,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the mind will have to.

81
00:07:30,954 --> 00:07:32,955
[SPEAKER_01]: It will have to turn away, because you're left with no choice.

82
00:07:34,703 --> 00:07:39,446
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, okay, so I don't need to keep my mind now like water or like, like, like water, like earth.

83
00:07:39,606 --> 00:07:40,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It is so.

84
00:07:40,967 --> 00:07:46,550
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not distract myself and start providing unsuitable food for that mind and it will stay.

85
00:07:46,970 --> 00:07:48,551
[SPEAKER_01]: It will stay in regard to the entire world.

86
00:07:49,772 --> 00:07:58,216
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, whatever people praise or blame, whichever they speak out of anger or kindness, it makes no difference.

87
00:07:58,276 --> 00:08:00,838
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind remains turned away from the entire thing anyway.

88
00:08:02,058 --> 00:08:06,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there's dispassion, because there is no more fueling of passion through your careless acts.

89
00:08:18,463 --> 00:08:29,766
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in terms of that, that other sutta, the Rāhula, when he's instructing Rāhula about, well, be like earth and

90
00:08:31,916 --> 00:08:33,897
[SPEAKER_03]: It has the Pali word sama, there.

91
00:08:34,597 --> 00:08:40,520
[SPEAKER_03]: So it's like, tune in to earth, you know, or balance that.

92
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:41,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what composure is.

93
00:08:42,761 --> 00:08:43,741
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what samādhi is.

94
00:08:43,841 --> 00:08:47,042
[SPEAKER_01]: It's tuning of the context rightly, attending to it rightly.

95
00:08:47,763 --> 00:08:49,463
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the measure of the tune?

96
00:08:50,724 --> 00:08:55,806
[SPEAKER_01]: In light of what I just said, what would be the measure of, oh, I... this is the right tune now?

97
00:08:56,326 --> 00:08:56,907
[SPEAKER_02]: Dispassion.

98
00:08:58,287 --> 00:09:00,248
[SPEAKER_03]: Not being affected by what's happening, basically.

99
00:09:00,955 --> 00:09:02,436
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind not being affected.

100
00:09:02,616 --> 00:09:02,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

101
00:09:02,997 --> 00:09:04,338
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind being turned away.

102
00:09:04,378 --> 00:09:05,398
[SPEAKER_01]: You start recognizing that.

103
00:09:06,279 --> 00:09:13,064
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that, by doing that sama rightly, composing it rightly, practicing samādhi rightly, i.e.

104
00:09:13,104 --> 00:09:29,237
[SPEAKER_01]: establishing that context, so, not on the level of abstract ideas, and certainly not on the level of physical sensation watching, but find the balance between the two, for lack of a better word, and then, gotta be patient with it, and see

105
00:09:30,055 --> 00:09:33,277
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you try to tune a string instrument.

106
00:09:33,617 --> 00:09:40,060
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just hitting the strings and not waiting for the feedback of the sound, how do you know whether to tighten or to loosen?

107
00:09:41,501 --> 00:09:49,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the feedback here, it's... Is mind turning away from the entire image, from the entire experience, from thinking and pondering?

108
00:09:51,686 --> 00:09:55,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, exactly, yeah, dispassion and so on, that would be the result of the mind turned away.

109
00:09:56,309 --> 00:09:57,950
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you would have then the same thoughts,

110
00:09:58,796 --> 00:10:02,478
[SPEAKER_01]: They would be inducive of passion or ill-will before.

111
00:10:03,018 --> 00:10:09,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the same thoughts are there, but there is no passion or ill-will, because the mind is not there with them, so to speak.

112
00:10:12,882 --> 00:10:15,363
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that samā aspect, the tuning.

113
00:10:16,824 --> 00:10:22,146
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you tune the mind directly, but you tune the context appropriately,

114
00:10:23,457 --> 00:10:30,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Establish it, make sure it's in use, protect it on the basis of virtue and guarding the senses, and the mind is turning away.

115
00:10:30,483 --> 00:10:34,727
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that element of faith is necessary as well in the beginning, because you don't have an immediate feedback.

116
00:10:37,349 --> 00:10:41,312
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, how you feel is certainly not a reliable feedback.

117
00:10:41,392 --> 00:10:42,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I feel great, this must be right.

118
00:10:42,793 --> 00:10:44,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I feel bad, this might be wrong.

119
00:10:44,234 --> 00:10:44,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Not necessarily.

120
00:10:47,193 --> 00:10:56,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes that initial turning away of the mind might feel very unpleasant, because that's what the mind was depending on, like withdrawing from the favorite food, because you don't see that it's harming you.

121
00:10:56,461 --> 00:11:03,827
[SPEAKER_01]: But first you gotta stop eating it, endure that desire to eat it again, and then you realize, oh, actually, yeah, look, I am getting better.

122
00:11:04,777 --> 00:11:24,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm lighter, I'm healthier, as a result of not eating, not letting mind sort of fall back and touch and take up all these other things that I used to attend to wrongly, as agreeable, non-harmful and so on.

123
00:11:28,196 --> 00:11:36,819
[SPEAKER_02]: One other way of describing the mind, in that way, is that it's greater in that...

124
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:52,946
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, yeah, it's broader, so that even if particular hindrances arise, it's, in relation to those hindrances, not pulled by them, it's able to... Well, yeah, yeah.

125
00:11:53,186 --> 00:11:55,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Theoretically speaking, that's correct.

126
00:11:55,187 --> 00:11:55,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said,

127
00:11:57,695 --> 00:12:06,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Even of itself, somebody, if they hear the instruction that mind is greater, mind is broader, they can make whatever they want out of it.

128
00:12:07,097 --> 00:12:19,279
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's... you have to, like, be careful and it's like, well, yes, you can use these descriptions, but you need to really know why the mind is turning away, why the mind... and it's on account of danger.

129
00:12:20,519 --> 00:12:27,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the gratification, danger and the escape in regard to pretty much anything that takes mind's interest, certainly in regard to sensual desire,

130
00:12:27,893 --> 00:12:28,874
[SPEAKER_01]: An unwholesome state.

131
00:12:29,414 --> 00:12:37,340
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can understand that if you've stopped acting towards it, if you stop distracting yourself, if you stop in sort of treading water around and ignoring that there is still tasks to be done.

132
00:12:37,440 --> 00:12:39,041
[SPEAKER_01]: All that thing that's before...

133
00:12:39,062 --> 00:12:40,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is all part of that development.

134
00:12:41,864 --> 00:12:54,033
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like some random observances that are done and then this is the, you know, the direct line to my enlightenment, this technique or this way of thinking or something like that.

135
00:12:55,959 --> 00:13:07,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly why for somebody all these things would be extremely mystical and abstract, and for somebody would be like the most acute description of the experience of jhāna, or how the mind turns away from sensuality, and so on.

136
00:13:08,665 --> 00:13:13,628
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference being in how much they've actually developed through the body, the speech, and then on the level of the thought.

137
00:13:16,517 --> 00:13:17,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we're back to what we said before.

138
00:13:17,878 --> 00:13:20,761
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you hear an instruction, it's, oh, wow, that sounds interesting.

139
00:13:21,341 --> 00:13:21,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

140
00:13:21,721 --> 00:13:25,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, find out more from that person, or if they say anything else, how you arrive at it.

141
00:13:27,086 --> 00:13:34,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, there is this pleasure when the mind is withdrawn, and it's magical, and it's just immeasurable mind, and it's just the absolute bliss.

142
00:13:34,652 --> 00:13:34,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

143
00:13:35,513 --> 00:13:36,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you do it?

144
00:13:38,135 --> 00:13:40,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if they say, well, you know, you just sit and

145
00:13:41,077 --> 00:13:46,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Focus on sensations, and get this pleasure, and then latch on to it, and look closer, and contemplate the nature.

146
00:13:47,380 --> 00:13:50,322
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's not really the impression you get when you read the suttas.

147
00:13:51,762 --> 00:13:52,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they say, how do you do it?

148
00:13:52,983 --> 00:14:01,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, keep the precepts, be fully withdrawn from unwholesome states, guard your sense doors, like your life depends on it, and then dwell in seclusion.

149
00:14:02,933 --> 00:14:13,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Up until the intentions become apparent, where the mind leans towards pleasure, leans away from pain, all these things, and then you have that bliss of withdrawal and stuff.

150
00:14:13,036 --> 00:14:17,958
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh yeah, yeah, that is, when I did the suttas, that's kind of what, what it says there.

151
00:14:18,999 --> 00:14:25,621
[SPEAKER_01]: But everything else, 99% of work leading up to that, okay, yeah, that's, that's, that's covered.

152
00:14:25,641 --> 00:14:29,462
[SPEAKER_01]: We all understand that, you know, preceptual, guiding of the senses.

153
00:14:30,022 --> 00:14:31,803
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the way to jhāna.

154
00:14:35,537 --> 00:14:37,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you left everything else out, that's not the way.

155
00:14:37,918 --> 00:14:48,701
[SPEAKER_01]: And if somebody had understood the way and had the jhāna, they would not be able to omit this way of getting there, because they would understand how they got there.

156
00:14:49,481 --> 00:14:51,342
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't stumble upon it accidentally.

157
00:14:52,275 --> 00:15:01,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the Buddha himself, when he remembered the experience of jhāna that he had when he was still a young... under the tree there with the father somewhere...

158
00:15:01,099 --> 00:15:01,999
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember the story.

159
00:15:02,659 --> 00:15:05,140
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, oh, I remember that experience.

160
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:06,681
[SPEAKER_01]: He obviously knew how he got there.

161
00:15:07,661 --> 00:15:11,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me try going that way again and see if that's the way to enlightenment.

162
00:15:13,363 --> 00:15:14,304
[SPEAKER_01]: And how you get there?

163
00:15:14,384 --> 00:15:17,125
[SPEAKER_01]: By complete withdrawal from unwholesome states.

164
00:15:19,019 --> 00:15:29,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind completely turned away from unwholesome phenomena of lust, greed, desire, aversion, dissatisfaction, five hindrances.

165
00:15:29,904 --> 00:15:31,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is different to trying to get rid of...

166
00:15:31,925 --> 00:15:33,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely different, yeah.

167
00:15:33,506 --> 00:15:40,770
[SPEAKER_01]: See, you can get rid of it, but you could attend to it rightly, so the mind turns away from it, while the thing remains.

168
00:15:44,025 --> 00:15:47,607
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are getting rid of it, means you are already... your mind is already connected with it.

169
00:15:47,667 --> 00:15:50,227
[SPEAKER_01]: It sees no other perspective in regard to it.

170
00:15:50,267 --> 00:15:52,308
